{
  "id": "copy-list-with-random-pointer",
  "questionId": "copy-list-with-random-pointer",
  "slug": "copy-list-with-random-pointer",
  "title": "Copy List with Random Pointer",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Linked list",
  "patterns": [
    "linked list",
    "hash map",
    "deep copy"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the hard part is that a random pointer might point to a node you haven't created a copy of yet - how can you guarantee every copy already exists when you need to wire up randoms?",
    "what if, before wiring anything up, you first created every copy node up front, so all of them already exist by the time you need to point to any of them?",
    "one classic trick: splice each copy node directly after its original in the same list (orig1 -> copy1 -> orig2 -> copy2 -> ...), so 'the copy of X' is always just 'X.next'",
    "with that interleaving, set each copy's random as original.random.next (the copy right after the original's random target), then unweave the list back into two separate lists: the original list restored, and the new copied list"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "setting a copy's random pointer to the original node instead of the corresponding copy node",
    "processing nodes before every copy exists, leading to random pointers referencing not-yet-created nodes",
    "forgetting to restore or properly detach the original list when using the interleaving trick, corrupting the original list's next pointers"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given `nodes`, an array describing a singly linked list where each node has an extra \"random\" pointer that can point to any node in the list (or to nothing):\n\n- the rows are given in the list's sequential order - row `i`'s \"next\" is always row `i+1`\n- row `i` of `nodes` is `[val, randomIndex]`: `val` is that node's value\n- `randomIndex` is the 0-indexed position within `nodes` of the node its random pointer points to, or `-1` if its random pointer points to nothing\n\n**Produce a deep copy of this list, a completely new set of nodes with the same values in the same order, where each copied node's random pointer points to the corresponding copied node (not the original), and return the copy in the exact same [val, randomIndex] row format.**\n\nSince the grader can only check the structure of your output (not object identity), a structurally identical copy, same values, same random indices, is exactly the correct and expected result.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nodes.length <= 1000",
      "-10^4 <= val <= 10^4",
      "randomIndex is either -1 or a valid 0-indexed position within nodes"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "an empty list",
      "a single node whose random pointer points to itself",
      "a single node with no random pointer (-1)",
      "every node's random pointer points to -1 (no randoms at all)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nodes = [[7, -1], [13, 0], [11, 4], [10, 2], [1, 0]]",
        "output": "[[7, -1], [13, 0], [11, 4], [10, 2], [1, 0]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[7, -1], [13, 0], [11, 4], [10, 2], [1, 0]]",
        "explanation": "the copy has the same values and the same random-pointer targets, just as new nodes"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nodes = [[1, 1], [2, 1]]",
        "output": "[[1, 1], [2, 1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1, 1], [2, 1]]",
        "explanation": "both nodes' random pointers point to node 1 (value 2) in both the original and the copy"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nodes = []",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "an empty list copies to an empty list"
      }
